zucrypt: copyright and licensing of all included files
======================================================

zucrypt itself
--------------

Everything outside src/vendor/ is Copyright (c) 2026 Pedro Baltazar and is
licensed under the MIT licence; see the LICENSE file at the top of the
package.

That includes the files that configure and adapt the vendored backend:

    src/zuc_crypto_config.h   the TF-PSA-Crypto build configuration, which
                              replaces upstream's psa/crypto_config.h
    src/zuc_random.c          the operating-system random source the backend
                              is configured to call out to
    src/zuc_backend.c         backend bring-up and version reporting
    src/zucrypt_r.c           the R entry points
    src/Makevars              the build, including the explicit object list


Third-party code: TF-PSA-Crypto
-------------------------------

    Location:  src/vendor/tf-psa-crypto/
    Upstream:  https://github.com/Mbed-TLS/TF-PSA-Crypto
    Release:   tf-psa-crypto-1.1.1
    Licence:   Apache-2.0
    Copyright: Copyright The Mbed TLS Contributors

TF-PSA-Crypto is published under a dual Apache-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-or-later
licence, the recipient choosing between them. zucrypt takes it under
Apache-2.0. The full text of both licences, as upstream ships it, is in
src/vendor/tf-psa-crypto/LICENSE in the source package, and is installed as
licenses/tf-psa-crypto-LICENSE in the installed package -- so that it travels
with the compiled backend, including inside libzucrypt.a when another package
links it (Apache-2.0 section 4(a)).

Apache-2.0 and MIT are compatible: each file keeps its own terms, and a
recipient of this package receives the vendored files under Apache-2.0 and
everything else under MIT.

Only part of the upstream release is included here. The vendored tree is the
dependency closure of the sources that carry a symbol under
src/zuc_crypto_config.h -- 109 files of an archive with several thousand. The
exact list is tools/vendor/keep/tf-psa-crypto.txt, the release and its
checksum are in tools/vendor/manifest.tsv, and tools/vendor/verify checks
offline that the tree, the manifest, the build configuration and this file all
agree.

Local changes are carried as patch files under tools/patches/<source>/ and
applied by tools/vendor/fetch; no upstream file is edited in place. Each patch
is listed in the manifest's `patches` column and named here, and
tools/vendor/verify fails if those three records disagree.

One patch is applied:

    tools/patches/tf-psa-crypto/0001-avoid-zero-size-pubkey-array.patch

        inc/mbedtls/pk.h declares a zero-size array when no public-key
        algorithm is enabled, which this build does not enable. Zero-size
        arrays are a GNU extension rather than ISO C, and GCC with -Wpedantic
        -- which CRAN's r-devel container passes -- reports it as a
        significant warning. The patch starts the length at 1 instead of 0.
        It changes nothing in a configuration that enables a public-key
        algorithm. The patch file carries the full rationale.
